# hello: "Hello, World!" above a Vasarely swell
#
# The top three rows are the program, snaking east-west-east: each character's
# code point is built in base 8 from the scale channel (PUSH the leading digit,
# then PUSH_ACC each following one, which computes v*8+digit), then printed with
# OUT_CHAR. 49 cells of code, and every push disc's size IS its digit -- so the
# program cannot follow the gradient. The code is where the sphere is missing.
#
# Everything below is a field of PUSH discs that never execute, their scales a
# radial gradient centred in the lower rows.
#
# Two form colours, not one: OUT_CHAR is cross variant 1, and a non-void cell
# needs variant != ground, so the plate cannot be cream-grounded. Black discs
# carry the pushes and the field; cream marks PUSH_ACC and every OUT_CHAR. The
# program picks itself out in the second colour without being asked to.
#
# Ends with a newline: push 1, push_acc 2 makes 10, then OUT_CHAR.
%plate 20x16

o112  o212  o2.2  +2.2  o112  o242  o252  +2.2  o112  o252  o242  +2.2  o112  o252  o242  +2.2  o112  o252  o272  v1.2
v1.2  o222  o262  o112  +2.2  o272  o252  o112  +2.2  o272  o222  o112  +2.2  o2.2  o142  +2.2  o242  o152  +2.2  <1.2
>1.2  +2.2  o112  o252  o242  +2.2  o112  o242  o242  +2.2  o142  o212  +2.2  o112  o222  +2.2  ...2  o112  o1.2  o1.2
